export const REPORTS = {
  "Review models, routes, tasks": "<b>Core app logic review: Flask microblog at /home/learner/checkout</b>\n\nI only read files and edited nothing. venv/bin/python tests.py passed all 4 tests in 0.25s. Installed versions: SQLAlchemy 2.0.23, Flask 3.0.0, Werkzeug 3.0.1, Flask-SQLAlchemy 3.1.1, rq 1.15.1, elasticsearch 8.11.0.\n\nPaths below are relative to /home/learner/checkout.\n\n<b>HIGH</b>\n\n1. <b>The export task can start before its Task row exists, so it crashes.</b> app/models.py:209-215 and app/main/routes.py:225-226, failing at app/tasks.py:20-21.\n   - <b>What's wrong:</b> launch_task puts the job on the RQ queue first, and the Task row is only committed later, in the route.\n   - <b>Failure:</b> A fast worker runs _set_task_progress(0) before that commit. db.session.get(Task, id) returns None, so task.user raises AttributeError. The except block calls _set_task_progress again, which raises the same error. The job fails, and the Task row then sits at complete=False forever. Every later page shows a \"0%\" or \"100%\" banner, and get_task_in_progress blocks any new export.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Create and commit the Task row (with a pre-generated job id) before enqueuing. Also make _set_task_progress handle a missing Task.\n\n2. <b>Error handling in the export task is broken.</b> app/tasks.py:28-56.\n   - (a) The except block calls _set_task_progress(100) and then the finally block calls it again: two commits and two notifications.\n   - (b) There is no db.session.rollback(). If the exception came from the DB, the commit inside _set_task_progress raises PendingRollbackError. That hides the original error and skips the app.logger.error call.\n   - (c) tasks.py:11-12 creates one module-level app and context for the whole worker. A session left in a failed state carries over to the next job in the same worker process.\n   - (d) Any failure, including no user found (line 30) and SMTP errors, marks the task 100% complete. The user is never told it failed.\n   - (e) Line 40 sleeps 5s per post. rq's default job timeout is 180s, so any user with more than ~36 posts hits a timeout. JobTimeoutException subclasses Exception (verified), so the except block catches it. Result: the task shows \"complete\" and no email is ever sent.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Roll back in the except block, drop the duplicate call, db.session.remove() per job, report failures to the user, remove the sleep (or set job_timeout), and commit progress less often.\n\n3. <b>Elasticsearch failures cause 500s, including after a successful save.</b>\n   - app/models.py:41-51 with app/search.py:10,16: after_commit indexes the post *after* the DB commit. If ES is down or slow, the post is saved but the user gets a 500. Indexing of the remaining objects is skipped, and session._changes is never reset.\n   - app/search.py:22: search() raises when ES is unreachable. It also raises NotFoundError when the post index doesn't exist yet, i.e. a fresh deploy where nobody has posted.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Wrap the ES calls in try/except and log the error. Search should return [], 0 (or flash \"search unavailable\"). Consider indexing from a background task.\n\n4. <b>/translate has several holes.</b> app/translate.py:14-21, app/main/routes.py:156-162.\n   - No timeout= on requests.post: a slow Microsoft endpoint ties up workers indefinitely.\n   - Connection errors and a non-JSON body from r.json() are not handled, so they become 500s.\n   - source_language and dest_language come from the client and are pasted into the query string unescaped. For example, dest_language=\"fr&amp;to=de&amp;to=ja...\" adds extra target languages, which multiplies translation cost on the shared key.\n   - There's no size limit on text and no rate limit, so any logged-in user can burn the translator quota.\n   - In the route, a missing JSON body (get_json() returns None) or missing keys cause a TypeError or KeyError and a 500.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Pass params={'from':..., 'to':...}, add timeout=(3, 10), check language codes against an allowlist, limit text length, and validate the input with a 400 response.\n\n<b>MEDIUM</b>\n\n5. <b>Search pagination crashes past the last page.</b> app/models.py:22-30, app/main/routes.py:170.\n   - <b>Page past the end:</b> page &gt; last page means ES returns total &gt; 0 with empty hits. db.case(*[], value=cls.id) then renders CASE id END (verified), which is invalid SQL, so the page 500s.\n   - <b>Page 0 or negative:</b> from_ becomes negative, ES returns a 400, and the page 500s.\n   - <b>Deep pages:</b> past ES max_result_window (10k results), ES errors.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Return [], total when ids is empty, and clamp page &gt;= 1.\n\n6. <b>before_commit misses objects that were already flushed.</b> app/models.py:33-38.\n   - <b>What's wrong:</b> It snapshots session.new/session.dirty at commit time. Anything autoflushed earlier in the request (a query after modifying a Post, or an explicit flush()) is no longer in those sets and never gets indexed or deleted.\n   - It also re-indexes Posts whose changes didn't touch a searchable field.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Collect changes in after_flush (accumulating across flushes) and clear them in after_commit and after_rollback.\n\n7. <b>Every authenticated request does a DB write and commit.</b> app/main/routes.py:16-22.\n   - <b>What's wrong:</b> before_app_request updates last_seen and commits on every request, including AJAX polling of /notifications and the /user/&lt;u&gt;/popup popovers.\n   - <b>Failure:</b> Constant write load and lock contention, especially on SQLite. If that commit fails (e.g. \"database is locked\"), every page 500s.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Only update when last_seen is older than about a minute, and skip static/XHR endpoints.\n\n8. <b>/export_posts changes state on a GET and can double-launch.</b> app/main/routes.py:219-227, link at app/templates/user.html:17.\n   - <b>CSRF:</b> It's a GET with no CSRF protection, so a third-party page (e.g. &lt;img src=.../export_posts&gt;) can trigger exports for a logged-in user.\n   - <b>Race:</b> The check-then-launch is unprotected: two quick clicks both pass get_task_in_progress and enqueue duplicate jobs.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Make it a POST with EmptyForm, and use a DB uniqueness constraint or a Redis lock per (user, task name).\n\n9. <b>Naive and aware datetimes are mixed.</b>\n   - <b>Where:</b> Columns are declared DateTime without timezone=True (app/models.py:106-111, 291, 310), yet the code writes aware datetime.now(timezone.utc) values to them. unread_message_count (models.py:196) compares against a naive datetime(1900,1,1).\n   - <b>Failure:</b> On SQLite the timezone is silently dropped. On PostgreSQL it depends on the session TimeZone: if that isn't UTC, stored values shift (uncertain, depends on the driver/server setting). Python code that compares a freshly created (aware) value with a DB-loaded (naive) value raises TypeError.\n   - tasks.py:39 appends 'Z' to isoformat(). That produces ...+00:00Z if the timestamp is aware (an object created in the same session).\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Use DateTime(timezone=True) consistently, or always store naive UTC, and format with .replace(tzinfo=utc).isoformat().\n\n10. <b>N+1 queries on every page.</b>\n    - app/templates/_post.html:4-11 lazy-loads post.author for each post on /index, /explore, /user and /search: up to 25 extra queries per page.\n    - <b>Fix:</b> Add .options(so.joinedload(Post.author)) in following_posts(), the explore query, and SearchableMixin.search.\n    - base.html:48,69 runs an unread count and a tasks query on every page, plus one Redis fetch per task in progress (get_progress).\n    - user.html runs 3 separate count queries.\n\n11. <b>Username edits are poorly validated.</b> app/main/routes.py:100-104, app/main/forms.py:12,21-26.\n    - The uniqueness check is check-then-insert, so two concurrent edits to the same name raise IntegrityError and a 500. There's no rollback or friendly error.\n    - There's no Length(max=64), so long names hit DataError on PostgreSQL.\n    - Any characters are allowed, and surrounding spaces are not stripped. A username containing / makes the profile unreachable: url_for builds /user/a/b and it 404s (verified).\n    - Lookalike names such as \"susan \" are possible.\n    - <b>Fix:</b> Add Length and Regexp validators and strip input; catch IntegrityError, then roll back and show a form error.\n\n12. <b>Little error handling for infrastructure failures.</b>\n    - app/__init__.py:43-44: if Redis is down, launch_task and Task.get_rq_job behave differently: get_rq_job catches RedisError, but enqueue in export_posts doesn't, so the route 500s.\n    - app/email.py:7-9: SMTP errors in the async email thread are never logged through app.logger, so they are effectively silent.\n\n<b>LOW</b>\n\n13. <b>Duplicate rows possible in follow()/unfollow().</b> app/models.py:144-150: these check then insert. Concurrent follows cause a primary-key IntegrityError and a 500. Low impact.\n\n14. <b>Notifications rely on client and server clocks.</b> app/models.py:329 and routes.py:233: notifications are keyed on the wall clock time(), with since supplied by the client. Clock skew between web and worker hosts, or two notifications in the same instant, can cause missed updates. A monotonically increasing id would be safer.\n\n15. <b>following_posts() is inefficient.</b> app/models.py:166-179: an outer join plus group_by(Post) groups on every column. An EXISTS subquery, or a UNION of own posts and followed posts, scales better.\n\n16. <b>Minor issues in send_message.</b>\n    - app/main/routes.py:181-196: users can message themselves.\n    - The recipient's unread count is calculated in the sender's request; last_message_read_time is naive vs aware (see item 9).\n\n17. <b>Hard-coded config values.</b>\n    - app/translate.py:12 hard-codes the translator region westus.\n    - config.py:9 falls back to SECRET_KEY='you-will-never-guess'; flagged for awareness, as config is out of scope.\n\n18. <b>app/cli.py:18-21 builds shell commands by string concatenation</b> (os.system('... -l ' + lang)). The input comes from the operator, so risk is low, but subprocess.run([...]) would be safer.\n\n19. <b>app/errors/handlers.py:7-9 returns JSON to clients that send */*</b> (e.g. curl), because both MIME types score equally. This is harmless.\n\n<b>Test coverage gaps (tests.py)</b>\n\nThe 4 tests cover only password hashing, avatar, follow/unfollow and following_posts. Not covered:\n- <b>Routes:</b> none at all — no Flask test client, so CSRF/POST-only follow/unfollow, edit_profile validation and messaging are untested.\n- <b>Models:</b> unread_message_count, add_notification replacement semantics, and launch_task/Task progress.\n- <b>Background jobs:</b> the export_posts task, including failure paths.\n- <b>Search:</b> SearchableMixin with ES mocked, ES missing/down, and the empty-page case.\n- <b>Translate:</b> translate with requests mocked, including timeouts and errors.\n- <b>Pagination:</b> edge cases such as page 0, a negative page, or a page past the end.\n- <b>Error handlers:</b> the JSON vs HTML 404/500 responses.\n\nThe tests also never exercise TestConfig with Redis; create_app builds a Redis client without connecting, which happens to work offline.",
  "Review deploy, templates, migrations": "<b>Review: deployment, config, templates, migrations, dependencies (read-only, no files changed)</b>\n\nRepo: /home/learner/checkout. I read every file in scope. I ran flask db heads and flask db history with the local venv, and diffed the translatable strings against the .po file.\n\n<b>HIGH</b>\n\n1. <b>Hardcoded fallback SECRET_KEY</b>, config.py:9: os.environ.get('SECRET_KEY') or 'you-will-never-guess'. If the env var isn't set, the app quietly uses a well-known key. That key signs the session cookies and the password-reset JWTs (models.py:181-191, HS256 over {'reset_password': id}). Anyone can then forge a session or reset token for any user id, which means full account takeover. Fix: fail at startup when SECRET_KEY is unset in non-debug/test mode, with no default.\n\n2. <b>Host-header poisoning of password-reset links</b>, deployment/nginx/microblog:13,27 (server_name _; plus proxy_set_header Host $host) together with config.py:10 (SERVER_NAME is optional). The reset emails build absolute links with url_for(..., _external=True) (app/templates/email/reset_password.html:7,12, reset_password.txt:5). An attacker requests a reset for a victim while sending Host: evil.com. The victim gets a real email whose link points at evil.com and gives away the token. Fix: give nginx an explicit server_name plus a default server that returns 444. Also require SERVER_NAME, or set Flask TRUSTED_HOSTS (Werkzeug 3.x).\n\n3. <b>Vulnerable pinned dependencies</b>, requirements.txt. I'm confident about these:\n   - Werkzeug==3.0.1 (line 53): CVE-2024-34069 (debugger RCE, fixed in 3.0.3) and CVE-2024-49766/49767 (fixed in 3.0.6). This is serious alongside .flaskenv FLASK_DEBUG=1 (MEDIUM #5).\n   - Jinja2==3.1.2 (line 29): CVE-2024-22195, CVE-2024-34064 (xmlattr), CVE-2024-56201/56326 (sandbox), CVE-2025-27516. Upgrade to 3.1.6 or later.\n   - gunicorn==21.2.0 (line 25): CVE-2024-1135 (request smuggling, fixed in 22.0.0) and CVE-2024-6827 (fixed in 23.0.0).\n   - requests==2.31.0 (line 44): CVE-2024-35195 (fixed in 2.32.0) and CVE-2024-47081 (.netrc leak, fixed in 2.32.4).\n   - urllib3==2.1.0 (line 52): CVE-2024-37891 (fixed in 2.2.2) and CVE-2025-50181/50182 (fixed in 2.5.0).\n   - idna==3.4 (line 27): CVE-2024-3651 (DoS, fixed in 3.7).\n   - certifi==2023.11.17 (line 7): CVE-2024-39689 (still trusts GLOBALTRUST).\n   - setuptools==68.2.2 (line 48): CVE-2024-6345 and CVE-2025-47273.\n   - dnspython==2.4.2 (line 11): CVE-2023-29483 (fixed in 2.6.1).\n   - aiosmtpd==1.4.4.post2 (line 1): CVE-2024-27305 and CVE-2024-34083 (SMTP smuggling). This is a dev tool in the production requirements.\n\n   Uncertain: I'm not sure whether Pygments==2.17.1 or Mako==1.3.0 have advisories. Flask==3.0.0 is not affected by CVE-2025-47278, which only affects 3.1.0.\n\n   Fix: upgrade all of these. Split dev tools (httpie, aiosmtpd, and the rich/pygments/markdown-it they pull in) into a separate dev requirements file, and add pip-audit to CI.\n\n<b>MEDIUM</b>\n\n4. <b>The Docker container runs as root with unpinned packages</b>, Dockerfile:1,5.\n   - FROM python:slim is a floating tag.\n   - No USER directive, so gunicorn and migrations run as root.\n   - Line 5 installs gunicorn pymysql cryptography unpinned. gunicorn is already pinned in requirements, so it's redundant there.\n   - No --no-cache-dir.\n\n   Fix: pin the base image (e.g. python:3.12-slim@sha256:...), pin the extra packages in requirements.txt, and add RUN useradd -m app and USER app. Layer ordering is fine: requirements are copied before the app code.\n\n5. <b>Debug mode committed</b>, .flaskenv:2 (FLASK_DEBUG=1). Any flask run or flask CLI use on a server picks up debug mode. With Werkzeug 3.0.1 that exposes the debugger (CVE-2024-34069). Fix: remove it from the committed .flaskenv and set it locally in .env.\n\n6. <b>.gitignore doesn't exclude secrets</b>, .gitignore (whole file). There's no .env, logs/, certs/ or *.pem. config.py:5 loads .env from the repo root, and the nginx config keeps the TLS key in the repo directory (deployment/nginx/microblog:16-17, /home/ubuntu/microblog/certs/key.pem). Either can be committed by accident. Fix: add .env, logs/, certs/ and *.pem.\n\n7. <b>No X-Forwarded-Proto, so reset links use http</b>, deployment/nginx/microblog:23-30. nginx doesn't send X-Forwarded-Proto. gunicorn would honour it from 127.0.0.1 by default, but without it, _external=True URLs in reset emails are http://, so the reset token travels in cleartext before the 301. The app also sets no SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE, REMEMBER_COOKIE_SECURE or SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE (config.py). Fix: add proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme; and set the secure cookie flags.\n\n8. <b>nginx TLS and headers are left at defaults</b>, deployment/nginx/microblog:12-17.\n   - No ssl_protocols or ssl_ciphers. Older nginx defaults still allow TLSv1 and 1.1.\n   - No HSTS.\n   - No X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options/frame-ancestors, Referrer-Policy or CSP.\n   - No http2.\n   - The cert is self-signed (comment at line 15).\n\n   Fix: set ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;, add add_header Strict-Transport-Security \"max-age=31536000\" always; plus the other headers, and use Let's Encrypt.\n\n9. <b>Hovering a username can trigger GET requests on the viewer's behalf (CSRF)</b>, app/templates/base.html:131: fetch('/user/' + ev.target.innerText.trim() + '/popup'). The username isn't URL-encoded, and usernames have no character restriction (app/auth/forms.py:18, app/main/forms.py:12). A user named ../auth/logout? or ../export_posts? makes everyone who hovers their name fire GET /auth/logout?/popup or /export_posts?/popup. Both are state-changing GET routes (auth/routes.py:33, main/routes.py:219). Fix: put the popup URL in a data- attribute built with url_for in _post.html, or use encodeURIComponent. Also restrict usernames to a safe regex and make logout/export POST with CSRF.\n\n10. <b>Popover renders raw HTML</b>, app/templates/base.html:121-122 (html: true, sanitize: false). The fetched popup HTML is injected unsanitized. The current user_popup.html autoescapes all user data, so this isn't exploitable today. But it removes the Bootstrap sanitizer as a backstop, and combined with #9 the fetched path is attacker-influenced. Fix: drop sanitize: false and extend allowList for form/input/button.\n\n11. <b>Procfile starts the app even if migrations fail</b>, Procfile:1. flask db upgrade; flask translate compile; gunicorn ... uses ;, so gunicorn starts against an un-migrated schema. Fix: chain with &amp;&amp;. Uncertain: Procfile:2 rq worker microblog-tasks doesn't pass -u $REDIS_URL. As far as I know the rq CLI reads RQ_REDIS_URL, not REDIS_URL, so the worker may connect to localhost.\n\n12. <b>boot.sh retries forever</b>, boot.sh:3-10. flask db upgrade is retried every 5s with no limit and no error output kept, so a bad migration or bad DATABASE_URL makes the container hang \"running\" instead of failing fast. Also, with DATABASE_URL unset, config.py:11-13 silently falls back to SQLite inside the container, so the migration \"succeeds\" and data is lost when the container goes away. Fix: cap retries (e.g. 10) then exit 1, and require DATABASE_URL in production.\n\n13. <b>Messages page pagination is broken</b>, app/templates/messages.html:11,16. Both links have href=\"#\" and ignore the prev_url/next_url that the route passes. Fix: use {{ prev_url }} and {{ next_url }} as index.html does.\n\n<b>LOW</b>\n\n14. <b>Fragile javascript: URL in _post.html:21-25.</b> post.language and g.locale are placed inside JS string literals in a javascript: href. HTML escaping doesn't protect here, because entities are decoded before the JS runs. The values are safe today (langdetect output and a whitelisted locale), but any future way to set language (e.g. a posts API) becomes XSS. Fix: use data- attributes and an event listener.\n\n15. <b>Translation strings render as raw HTML</b>, _post.html:14-15. Under newstyle gettext with autoescape the translated string is treated as Markup, so translator-supplied HTML in .po files is rendered unescaped. The interpolated args are still escaped. This is the accepted pattern; just keep .po files trusted.\n\n16. <b>config.py:17 MAIL_USE_TLS</b>: is not None means MAIL_USE_TLS=false or 0 turns TLS on. Parse it as a boolean. Also config.py:20 ADMINS is a placeholder address that is used as the email sender and the error-mail recipient.\n\n17. <b>Logs go to a file inside the container.</b> config.py:14 and app/__init__.py:78-91: unless LOG_TO_STDOUT is set, logs are written to logs/ inside the container, and the Dockerfile doesn't set it. Fix: ENV LOG_TO_STDOUT=1 in the Dockerfile. The legacy ENV FLASK_APP microblog.py syntax at Dockerfile:12 should become ENV FLASK_APP=microblog.py.\n\n18. <b>nginx static location</b>, deployment/nginx/microblog:32-34. location /static without a trailing slash also matches /staticXYZ. It isn't traversable as written, because neither the location nor the alias has a trailing slash. Prefer location /static/ { alias .../app/static/; }.\n\n19. <b>Supervisor configs</b>, deployment/supervisor/*.conf. No stdout/stderr log settings, gunicorn has no access log, and the tasks worker has no stopwaitsecs for in-flight jobs. Minor.\n\n20. <b>Misleading migration docstring</b>, migrations/versions/d049de007ccf_private_messages.py:4. The docstring says Revises: 834b1a697901 but the code (line 14) says down_revision = '2b017edaa91f'. The chain is linear with a single head, 834b1a697901 (verified with flask db history). But if someone \"fixes\" the code to match the docstring, it creates a cycle (834b → c81b → f7ac → d049 → 834b). Fix the docstring.\n\n21. <b>Migration style and indexes.</b>\n    - 834b1a697901_user_tokens.py:21-31 uses plain op.add_column/drop_column while every other migration uses batch_alter_table. Downgrade on SQLite needs 3.35 or later. Use batch mode for consistency.\n    - Down-migrations otherwise correctly mirror their upgrades.\n    - Nullability and lengths match models.py.\n    - Performance: followers.followed_id and task.user_id have no index (in the model or migrations). task is queried on every authenticated page render (base.html:69), and followed_id in follower counts. Consider adding indexes.\n\n22. <b>Translations</b>, app/translations/es/LC_MESSAGES/messages.po:\n    - \"Search Results\" (line 227) has an empty msgstr, so it shows in English.\n    - Three msgids are stale and unused: \"Previous results\", \"Next results\", \"Error: Could not contact server.\" (lines 230-236, 201-203).\n    - All #: location comments are out of date (POT from 2017).\n\n    Every string used in code or templates has an entry. Fix: run flask translate update and fill in the missing translation. Note .mo files are gitignored, so non-Docker deploys (supervisor) must run flask translate compile. Also, app/cli.py:18-39 uses os.system with string concatenation of lang (command injection, but only from a CLI argument, so minimal risk).\n\n23. <b>Template odds and ends.</b>\n    - base.html:2 hardcodes lang=\"en\"; it should be {{ g.locale }}.\n    - The logout link at base.html:60 is a GET, so logout can be triggered cross-site.\n    - In bootstrap_wtf.html:57, hidden-field errors (including a CSRF-token failure) use invalid-feedback, which is display:none without a sibling .is-invalid, so the user never sees them.\n\n    CSRF tokens themselves are present: every POST form uses quick_form (form.hidden_tag()) or explicit hidden_tag() (user.html:22,29, user_popup.html:14,21). Search is GET. The /translate fetch (base.html:101) sends no CSRF token and there's no global CSRFProtect, but get_json() needs application/json, which forces a CORS preflight, so cross-site abuse is unlikely.\n\n24. <b>requirements.txt:37</b>: psycopg2-binary is discouraged for production by its maintainers. Flask-Mail==0.9.1 is old and largely unmaintained. Low.\n\n<b>No issues found in</b>\n- Vagrantfile: just a box and a private IP, no provisioning.\n- .gitattributes: forces LF on *.sh, which is good for boot.sh.\n- migrations/env.py and alembic.ini: standard Flask-Migrate setup.\n- boot.sh's exec gunicorn: correct.\n- Email templates: autoescaped, and the .txt versions are fine.",
  "Review API and auth": "Code review: auth and REST API of the microblog app at /home/learner/checkout (read-only, no repo files edited)\n\n<b>Tests:</b> pytest is not installed in the venv. venv/bin/python tests.py gives 4/4 passing (UserModelCase only). There are no tests for auth or the API. (python -I tests.py cannot import app, because -I leaves the script dir off sys.path.) I also checked the findings below with the Flask test client against a throwaway DB under /tmp, which I deleted afterwards. Where a finding says \"confirmed\", I saw it happen in that run.\n\n<b>HIGH</b>\n\n1. <b>Hardcoded fallback SECRET_KEY</b> (config.py:9, 'you-will-never-guess')\n   - <b>Problem:</b> If the SECRET_KEY env var is unset, anyone can sign valid JWTs and session cookies.\n   - <b>Scenario:</b> An attacker signs {'reset_password': &lt;id&gt;, 'exp': ...} with the known key, opens /auth/reset_password/&lt;jwt&gt; and sets a new password for any user ID. That is account takeover. They can also forge the Flask session _user_id cookie. Confirmed: a forged reset JWT opened the reset form (200).\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Fail at startup if SECRET_KEY is missing outside dev/testing. Never ship a default.\n\n2. <b>Open redirect via next</b> (app/auth/routes.py:26-29)\n   - <b>Problem:</b> The only check is urlsplit(next).netloc != ''. That misses backslash and scheme-only forms. Confirmed redirects after login:\n     - next=/\\evil.com → Location: /\\evil.com (browsers treat it as //evil.com)\n     - next=\\\\evil.com → \\\\evil.com\n     - next=https:evil.com → https:evil.com\n   - <b>Scenario:</b> Phishing link https://site/auth/login?next=/%5Cevil.com. The user logs in on the real site and lands on the attacker's page.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Require next to start with a single /, not // or /\\. Reject any backslash, and require an empty scheme and netloc. Or allowlist endpoints / use url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme-style logic.\n\n<b>MEDIUM</b>\n\n3. <b>Password reset token can be replayed and is not tied to the password</b> (app/models.py:181-193, app/auth/routes.py:72-84)\n   - <b>Problem:</b> The JWT holds only the user id and exp. It stays valid for its full 10 minutes after use. Confirmed: the same token reset the password twice, and the second password took effect.\n   - <b>Scenario:</b> A leaked reset link (mail logs, browser history, Referer to the jsdelivr CDN loaded in base.html:90) can be reused after the victim has already reset.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Put a fingerprint of the current password_hash (or a per-user nonce) in the token and check it on verify. Use jwt.decode(..., options={\"require\": [\"exp\"]}) and narrow except Exception to jwt.InvalidTokenError.\n\n4. <b>Password reset does not revoke API tokens</b> (app/auth/routes.py:81-82)\n   - <b>Problem:</b> set_password does not call revoke_token(), and nothing invalidates existing sessions or remember-me cookies.\n   - <b>Scenario:</b> A victim resets after a compromise, but the attacker's bearer token stays valid until it expires (up to 1h), and their sessions keep working.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Call user.revoke_token() on reset. Use a session/alternative-id rotation for Flask-Login.\n\n5. <b>API create_user crashes on malformed input and does no validation</b> (app/api/users.py:47-57, app/models.py:252-257)\n   - <b>500 errors (confirmed):</b> a JSON null body, a JSON list body ([\"username\",\"email\",\"password\"]), and a non-string password ({\"password\": 1}). The cause is 'x' in None and generate_password_hash(int).\n   - <b>Accepted without validation (confirmed 201):</b> empty username, empty password, and email:\"not-an-email\".\n   - <b>No length or type checks:</b> these fields are limited only by the DB (String(64)/(120)/(140); SQLite doesn't enforce). For example, about_me of 500 chars was accepted.\n   - <b>Scenario:</b> Anyone without auth can create accounts with empty passwords or junk emails, and can trigger 500s.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Use request.get_json(silent=True) and require a dict. Validate types, lengths, email format and a non-empty password (or reuse a schema/WTForms). Return 400 on failure.\n\n6. <b>API update_user accepts nulls and bad types</b> (app/api/users.py:70-80)\n   - <b>Problem:</b> {\"username\": null} gives a 500 (confirmed; IntegrityError / crash). There is no type, length or email validation, and an email can be changed without re-verification.\n   - <b>Race:</b> The uniqueness checks at lines 71-78 are check-then-act. Two concurrent requests both pass, and the unique index raises IntegrityError, which returns 500 instead of 400. The same race exists in create_user.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Validate the same way as in #5. Catch IntegrityError, roll back and return 400/409.\n   - <b>Authorization:</b> The A-modifies-B check is correct (users.py:67-68 returns 403 when the token user != id). Mass assignment is properly limited to username/email/about_me, and the password is ignored on update.\n\n7. <b>Unauthenticated email and username enumeration</b> (app/api/users.py:50-55)\n   - <b>Problem:</b> POST /api/users with no auth returns \"please use a different email address\" for registered emails (confirmed). This undoes the enumeration-safe wording in reset_password_request (routes.py:63-66). The registration form does the same (forms.py:26-36).\n   - <b>Timing leaks:</b> verify_password (api/auth.py:13-14) and login (routes.py:22) only run the expensive hash check when the user exists, so response time reveals valid usernames.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Rate-limit registration. Run a dummy check_password_hash when the user is missing. Consider sending a \"you already have an account\" email instead of an inline error.\n\n8. <b>No rate limiting or lockout on credential endpoints</b>\n   - <b>Where:</b> /api/tokens (Basic auth), /auth/login, /api/users, /auth/reset_password_request.\n   - <b>Scenario:</b> Unlimited password brute force, and reset-email flooding of any address.\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Add Flask-Limiter or similar.\n\n<b>LOW</b>\n\n9. <b>Pagination metadata reflects raw input</b> (app/api/users.py:19-20 and similar; app/models.py:66-82)\n   - <b>Problem:</b> page=-3&amp;per_page=0 returns 200 with _meta.page=-3, per_page=0. Flask-SQLAlchemy quietly uses page 1 / per_page 20, so the metadata and self link are wrong (confirmed). Negative per_page also passes min(...,100).\n   - <b>Fix:</b> Clamp page&gt;=1 and 1&lt;=per_page&lt;=100, or report resources.page / resources.per_page.\n\n10. <b>API errors not always JSON</b> (app/api/errors.py:17-19)\n    - <b>Problem:</b> Blueprint error handlers don't see routing errors. A 405 on /api/tokens (GET) returned an HTML body (confirmed). App-level handlers (app/errors/handlers.py) only cover 404/500, and use Accept-header sniffing rather than the path.\n    - <b>Smaller issues:</b> handle_exception drops e.description. A 500 from an API route returns JSON only if the client's Accept prefers JSON.\n    - <b>Fix:</b> Add an app-level HTTPException handler that returns JSON when request.blueprint == 'api' or the path starts with /api/.\n\n11. <b>Bearer tokens stored in plaintext</b> (app/models.py:109-110, 264)\n    - <b>Problem:</b> Tokens are stored as is and looked up by equality in SQL (models.py:275).\n    - <b>Scenario:</b> A DB read or backup leak exposes live tokens.\n    - <b>Fix:</b> Store a SHA-256 of the token and look up by the hash. The timing side channel from SQL equality is negligible, so not a concern.\n\n12. <b>get_token returns the existing token without extending it</b> (models.py:261-263)\n    - <b>Problem:</b> If the current token has more than 60s left, the same token is returned with its original expiry. That is correct but surprising: clients that re-auth get a token that may die soon.\n    - <b>Revocation works:</b> confirmed 401 after DELETE /api/tokens, and the next POST issues a new token.\n    - <b>Uncertain:</b> whether this is intended behaviour.\n\n13. <b>check_password crashes when password_hash is NULL</b> (models.py:137-138)\n    - <b>Problem:</b> The column is Optional. check_password_hash(None, ...) raises, so login or Basic auth for such a user returns 500.\n    - <b>Uncertain:</b> No current code path creates users without a password. Guard with if not self.password_hash: return False.\n\n14. <b>Logout via GET</b> (routes.py:33-36)\n    - <b>Problem:</b> A cross-site image tag or link can log users out (CSRF logout). Minor.\n    - <b>Fix:</b> Use POST with a CSRF token.\n\n15. <b>Insecure cookie defaults</b> (config.py)\n    - <b>Problem:</b> SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE, REMEMBER_COOKIE_SECURE and SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE are not set, and the remember-me cookie lasts 365 days by default.\n    - <b>Fix:</b> Set them for production.\n\n16. <b>Case-sensitive username and email uniqueness and lookup</b> (forms.py:27-35, routes.py:61-62, api/users.py:50-55)\n    - <b>Problem:</b> A@x.com and a@x.com can be separate accounts, and a reset request with different casing silently finds no user.\n    - <b>Fix:</b> Normalise to lowercase (at least emails) on write and on lookup.\n\n<b>Checked and OK</b>\n- <b>Templates:</b> No injection or open redirect in app/templates/auth/* or email/reset_password.*. Jinja autoescape covers user.username in the HTML email. The reset URL is built with url_for(_external=True); behind a proxy without SERVER_NAME it uses the Host header (host-header poisoning of the reset link is possible if the deployment doesn't pin SERVER_NAME or trusted hosts — uncertain, deployment-dependent).\n- <b>Email exposure:</b> to_dict never includes email in API responses.\n- <b>JWT algorithm:</b> It is pinned (algorithms=['HS256']), so no alg-confusion attack.\n- <b>Token generation:</b> secrets.token_hex(16) is adequate."
};
export const REPORTS_TEXT = {
  "Review models, routes, tasks": "## Core app logic review: Flask microblog at /home/learner/checkout\n\nI only read files and edited nothing. venv/bin/python tests.py passed all 4 tests in 0.25s. Installed versions: SQLAlchemy 2.0.23, Flask 3.0.0, Werkzeug 3.0.1, Flask-SQLAlchemy 3.1.1, rq 1.15.1, elasticsearch 8.11.0.\n\nPaths below are relative to /home/learner/checkout.\n\n### HIGH\n\n1. The export task can start before its Task row exists, so it crashes. app/models.py:209-215 and app/main/routes.py:225-226, failing at app/tasks.py:20-21.\n   - What's wrong: launch_task puts the job on the RQ queue first, and the Task row is only committed later, in the route.\n   - Failure: A fast worker runs _set_task_progress(0) before that commit. db.session.get(Task, id) returns None, so task.user raises AttributeError. The except block calls _set_task_progress again, which raises the same error. The job fails, and the Task row then sits at complete=False forever. Every later page shows a \"0%\" or \"100%\" banner, and get_task_in_progress blocks any new export.\n   - Fix: Create and commit the Task row (with a pre-generated job id) before enqueuing. Also make _set_task_progress handle a missing Task.\n\n2. Error handling in the export task is broken. app/tasks.py:28-56.\n   - (a) The except block calls _set_task_progress(100) and then the finally block calls it again: two commits and two notifications.\n   - (b) There is no db.session.rollback(). If the exception came from the DB, the commit inside _set_task_progress raises PendingRollbackError. That hides the original error and skips the app.logger.error call.\n   - (c) tasks.py:11-12 creates one module-level app and context for the whole worker. A session left in a failed state carries over to the next job in the same worker process.\n   - (d) Any failure, including no user found (line 30) and SMTP errors, marks the task 100% complete. The user is never told it failed.\n   - (e) Line 40 sleeps 5s per post. rq's default job timeout is 180s, so any user with more than ~36 posts hits a timeout. JobTimeoutException subclasses Exception (verified), so the except block catches it. Result: the task shows \"complete\" and no email is ever sent.\n   - Fix: Roll back in the except block, drop the duplicate call, db.session.remove() per job, report failures to the user, remove the sleep (or set job_timeout), and commit progress less often.\n\n3. Elasticsearch failures cause 500s, including after a successful save.\n   - app/models.py:41-51 with app/search.py:10,16: after_commit indexes the post *after* the DB commit. If ES is down or slow, the post is saved but the user gets a 500. Indexing of the remaining objects is skipped, and session._changes is never reset.\n   - app/search.py:22: search() raises when ES is unreachable. It also raises NotFoundError when the post index doesn't exist yet, i.e. a fresh deploy where nobody has posted.\n   - Fix: Wrap the ES calls in try/except and log the error. Search should return [], 0 (or flash \"search unavailable\"). Consider indexing from a background task.\n\n4. /translate has several holes. app/translate.py:14-21, app/main/routes.py:156-162.\n   - No timeout= on requests.post: a slow Microsoft endpoint ties up workers indefinitely.\n   - Connection errors and a non-JSON body from r.json() are not handled, so they become 500s.\n   - source_language and dest_language come from the client and are pasted into the query string unescaped. For example, dest_language=\"fr&to=de&to=ja...\" adds extra target languages, which multiplies translation cost on the shared key.\n   - There's no size limit on text and no rate limit, so any logged-in user can burn the translator quota.\n   - In the route, a missing JSON body (get_json() returns None) or missing keys cause a TypeError or KeyError and a 500.\n   - Fix: Pass params={'from':..., 'to':...}, add timeout=(3, 10), check language codes against an allowlist, limit text length, and validate the input with a 400 response.\n\n### MEDIUM\n\n5. Search pagination crashes past the last page. app/models.py:22-30, app/main/routes.py:170.\n   - Page past the end: page > last page means ES returns total > 0 with empty hits. db.case(*[], value=cls.id) then renders CASE id END (verified), which is invalid SQL, so the page 500s.\n   - Page 0 or negative: from_ becomes negative, ES returns a 400, and the page 500s.\n   - Deep pages: past ES max_result_window (10k results), ES errors.\n   - Fix: Return [], total when ids is empty, and clamp page >= 1.\n\n6. before_commit misses objects that were already flushed. app/models.py:33-38.\n   - What's wrong: It snapshots session.new/session.dirty at commit time. Anything autoflushed earlier in the request (a query after modifying a Post, or an explicit flush()) is no longer in those sets and never gets indexed or deleted.\n   - It also re-indexes Posts whose changes didn't touch a searchable field.\n   - Fix: Collect changes in after_flush (accumulating across flushes) and clear them in after_commit and after_rollback.\n\n7. Every authenticated request does a DB write and commit. app/main/routes.py:16-22.\n   - What's wrong: before_app_request updates last_seen and commits on every request, including AJAX polling of /notifications and the /user/<u>/popup popovers.\n   - Failure: Constant write load and lock contention, especially on SQLite. If that commit fails (e.g. \"database is locked\"), every page 500s.\n   - Fix: Only update when last_seen is older than about a minute, and skip static/XHR endpoints.\n\n8. /export_posts changes state on a GET and can double-launch. app/main/routes.py:219-227, link at app/templates/user.html:17.\n   - CSRF: It's a GET with no CSRF protection, so a third-party page (e.g. <img src=.../export_posts>) can trigger exports for a logged-in user.\n   - Race: The check-then-launch is unprotected: two quick clicks both pass get_task_in_progress and enqueue duplicate jobs.\n   - Fix: Make it a POST with EmptyForm, and use a DB uniqueness constraint or a Redis lock per (user, task name).\n\n9. Naive and aware datetimes are mixed.\n   - Where: Columns are declared DateTime without timezone=True (app/models.py:106-111, 291, 310), yet the code writes aware datetime.now(timezone.utc) values to them. unread_message_count (models.py:196) compares against a naive datetime(1900,1,1).\n   - Failure: On SQLite the timezone is silently dropped. On PostgreSQL it depends on the session TimeZone: if that isn't UTC, stored values shift (uncertain, depends on the driver/server setting). Python code that compares a freshly created (aware) value with a DB-loaded (naive) value raises TypeError.\n   - tasks.py:39 appends 'Z' to isoformat(). That produces ...+00:00Z if the timestamp is aware (an object created in the same session).\n   - Fix: Use DateTime(timezone=True) consistently, or always store naive UTC, and format with .replace(tzinfo=utc).isoformat().\n\n10. N+1 queries on every page.\n    - app/templates/_post.html:4-11 lazy-loads post.author for each post on /index, /explore, /user and /search: up to 25 extra queries per page.\n    - Fix: Add .options(so.joinedload(Post.author)) in following_posts(), the explore query, and SearchableMixin.search.\n    - base.html:48,69 runs an unread count and a tasks query on every page, plus one Redis fetch per task in progress (get_progress).\n    - user.html runs 3 separate count queries.\n\n11. Username edits are poorly validated. app/main/routes.py:100-104, app/main/forms.py:12,21-26.\n    - The uniqueness check is check-then-insert, so two concurrent edits to the same name raise IntegrityError and a 500. There's no rollback or friendly error.\n    - There's no Length(max=64), so long names hit DataError on PostgreSQL.\n    - Any characters are allowed, and surrounding spaces are not stripped. A username containing / makes the profile unreachable: url_for builds /user/a/b and it 404s (verified).\n    - Lookalike names such as \"susan \" are possible.\n    - Fix: Add Length and Regexp validators and strip input; catch IntegrityError, then roll back and show a form error.\n\n12. Little error handling for infrastructure failures.\n    - app/__init__.py:43-44: if Redis is down, launch_task and Task.get_rq_job behave differently: get_rq_job catches RedisError, but enqueue in export_posts doesn't, so the route 500s.\n    - app/email.py:7-9: SMTP errors in the async email thread are never logged through app.logger, so they are effectively silent.\n\n### LOW\n\n13. Duplicate rows possible in follow()/unfollow(). app/models.py:144-150: these check then insert. Concurrent follows cause a primary-key IntegrityError and a 500. Low impact.\n\n14. Notifications rely on client and server clocks. app/models.py:329 and routes.py:233: notifications are keyed on the wall clock time(), with since supplied by the client. Clock skew between web and worker hosts, or two notifications in the same instant, can cause missed updates. A monotonically increasing id would be safer.\n\n15. following_posts() is inefficient. app/models.py:166-179: an outer join plus group_by(Post) groups on every column. An EXISTS subquery, or a UNION of own posts and followed posts, scales better.\n\n16. Minor issues in send_message.\n    - app/main/routes.py:181-196: users can message themselves.\n    - The recipient's unread count is calculated in the sender's request; last_message_read_time is naive vs aware (see item 9).\n\n17. Hard-coded config values.\n    - app/translate.py:12 hard-codes the translator region westus.\n    - config.py:9 falls back to SECRET_KEY='you-will-never-guess'; flagged for awareness, as config is out of scope.\n\n18. app/cli.py:18-21 builds shell commands by string concatenation (os.system('... -l ' + lang)). The input comes from the operator, so risk is low, but subprocess.run([...]) would be safer.\n\n19. app/errors/handlers.py:7-9 returns JSON to clients that send */* (e.g. curl), because both MIME types score equally. This is harmless.\n\n### Test coverage gaps (tests.py)\n\nThe 4 tests cover only password hashing, avatar, follow/unfollow and following_posts. Not covered:\n- Routes: none at all — no Flask test client, so CSRF/POST-only follow/unfollow, edit_profile validation and messaging are untested.\n- Models: unread_message_count, add_notification replacement semantics, and launch_task/Task progress.\n- Background jobs: the export_posts task, including failure paths.\n- Search: SearchableMixin with ES mocked, ES missing/down, and the empty-page case.\n- Translate: translate with requests mocked, including timeouts and errors.\n- Pagination: edge cases such as page 0, a negative page, or a page past the end.\n- Error handlers: the JSON vs HTML 404/500 responses.\n\nThe tests also never exercise TestConfig with Redis; create_app builds a Redis client without connecting, which happens to work offline.",
  "Review deploy, templates, migrations": "## Review: deployment, config, templates, migrations, dependencies (read-only, no files changed)\n\nRepo: /home/learner/checkout. I read every file in scope. I ran flask db heads and flask db history with the local venv, and diffed the translatable strings against the .po file.\n\n### HIGH\n\n1. Hardcoded fallback SECRET_KEY, config.py:9: os.environ.get('SECRET_KEY') or 'you-will-never-guess'. If the env var isn't set, the app quietly uses a well-known key. That key signs the session cookies and the password-reset JWTs (models.py:181-191, HS256 over {'reset_password': id}). Anyone can then forge a session or reset token for any user id, which means full account takeover. Fix: fail at startup when SECRET_KEY is unset in non-debug/test mode, with no default.\n\n2. Host-header poisoning of password-reset links, deployment/nginx/microblog:13,27 (server_name _; plus proxy_set_header Host $host) together with config.py:10 (SERVER_NAME is optional). The reset emails build absolute links with url_for(..., _external=True) (app/templates/email/reset_password.html:7,12, reset_password.txt:5). An attacker requests a reset for a victim while sending Host: evil.com. The victim gets a real email whose link points at evil.com and gives away the token. Fix: give nginx an explicit server_name plus a default server that returns 444. Also require SERVER_NAME, or set Flask TRUSTED_HOSTS (Werkzeug 3.x).\n\n3. Vulnerable pinned dependencies, requirements.txt. I'm confident about these:\n   - Werkzeug==3.0.1 (line 53): CVE-2024-34069 (debugger RCE, fixed in 3.0.3) and CVE-2024-49766/49767 (fixed in 3.0.6). This is serious alongside .flaskenv FLASK_DEBUG=1 (MEDIUM #5).\n   - Jinja2==3.1.2 (line 29): CVE-2024-22195, CVE-2024-34064 (xmlattr), CVE-2024-56201/56326 (sandbox), CVE-2025-27516. Upgrade to 3.1.6 or later.\n   - gunicorn==21.2.0 (line 25): CVE-2024-1135 (request smuggling, fixed in 22.0.0) and CVE-2024-6827 (fixed in 23.0.0).\n   - requests==2.31.0 (line 44): CVE-2024-35195 (fixed in 2.32.0) and CVE-2024-47081 (.netrc leak, fixed in 2.32.4).\n   - urllib3==2.1.0 (line 52): CVE-2024-37891 (fixed in 2.2.2) and CVE-2025-50181/50182 (fixed in 2.5.0).\n   - idna==3.4 (line 27): CVE-2024-3651 (DoS, fixed in 3.7).\n   - certifi==2023.11.17 (line 7): CVE-2024-39689 (still trusts GLOBALTRUST).\n   - setuptools==68.2.2 (line 48): CVE-2024-6345 and CVE-2025-47273.\n   - dnspython==2.4.2 (line 11): CVE-2023-29483 (fixed in 2.6.1).\n   - aiosmtpd==1.4.4.post2 (line 1): CVE-2024-27305 and CVE-2024-34083 (SMTP smuggling). This is a dev tool in the production requirements.\n\n   Uncertain: I'm not sure whether Pygments==2.17.1 or Mako==1.3.0 have advisories. Flask==3.0.0 is not affected by CVE-2025-47278, which only affects 3.1.0.\n\n   Fix: upgrade all of these. Split dev tools (httpie, aiosmtpd, and the rich/pygments/markdown-it they pull in) into a separate dev requirements file, and add pip-audit to CI.\n\n### MEDIUM\n\n4. The Docker container runs as root with unpinned packages, Dockerfile:1,5.\n   - FROM python:slim is a floating tag.\n   - No USER directive, so gunicorn and migrations run as root.\n   - Line 5 installs gunicorn pymysql cryptography unpinned. gunicorn is already pinned in requirements, so it's redundant there.\n   - No --no-cache-dir.\n\n   Fix: pin the base image (e.g. python:3.12-slim@sha256:...), pin the extra packages in requirements.txt, and add RUN useradd -m app and USER app. Layer ordering is fine: requirements are copied before the app code.\n\n5. Debug mode committed, .flaskenv:2 (FLASK_DEBUG=1). Any flask run or flask CLI use on a server picks up debug mode. With Werkzeug 3.0.1 that exposes the debugger (CVE-2024-34069). Fix: remove it from the committed .flaskenv and set it locally in .env.\n\n6. .gitignore doesn't exclude secrets, .gitignore (whole file). There's no .env, logs/, certs/ or *.pem. config.py:5 loads .env from the repo root, and the nginx config keeps the TLS key in the repo directory (deployment/nginx/microblog:16-17, /home/ubuntu/microblog/certs/key.pem). Either can be committed by accident. Fix: add .env, logs/, certs/ and *.pem.\n\n7. No X-Forwarded-Proto, so reset links use http, deployment/nginx/microblog:23-30. nginx doesn't send X-Forwarded-Proto. gunicorn would honour it from 127.0.0.1 by default, but without it, _external=True URLs in reset emails are http://, so the reset token travels in cleartext before the 301. The app also sets no SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE, REMEMBER_COOKIE_SECURE or SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE (config.py). Fix: add proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme; and set the secure cookie flags.\n\n8. nginx TLS and headers are left at defaults, deployment/nginx/microblog:12-17.\n   - No ssl_protocols or ssl_ciphers. Older nginx defaults still allow TLSv1 and 1.1.\n   - No HSTS.\n   - No X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options/frame-ancestors, Referrer-Policy or CSP.\n   - No http2.\n   - The cert is self-signed (comment at line 15).\n\n   Fix: set ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;, add add_header Strict-Transport-Security \"max-age=31536000\" always; plus the other headers, and use Let's Encrypt.\n\n9. Hovering a username can trigger GET requests on the viewer's behalf (CSRF), app/templates/base.html:131: fetch('/user/' + ev.target.innerText.trim() + '/popup'). The username isn't URL-encoded, and usernames have no character restriction (app/auth/forms.py:18, app/main/forms.py:12). A user named ../auth/logout? or ../export_posts? makes everyone who hovers their name fire GET /auth/logout?/popup or /export_posts?/popup. Both are state-changing GET routes (auth/routes.py:33, main/routes.py:219). Fix: put the popup URL in a data- attribute built with url_for in _post.html, or use encodeURIComponent. Also restrict usernames to a safe regex and make logout/export POST with CSRF.\n\n10. Popover renders raw HTML, app/templates/base.html:121-122 (html: true, sanitize: false). The fetched popup HTML is injected unsanitized. The current user_popup.html autoescapes all user data, so this isn't exploitable today. But it removes the Bootstrap sanitizer as a backstop, and combined with #9 the fetched path is attacker-influenced. Fix: drop sanitize: false and extend allowList for form/input/button.\n\n11. Procfile starts the app even if migrations fail, Procfile:1. flask db upgrade; flask translate compile; gunicorn ... uses ;, so gunicorn starts against an un-migrated schema. Fix: chain with &&. Uncertain: Procfile:2 rq worker microblog-tasks doesn't pass -u $REDIS_URL. As far as I know the rq CLI reads RQ_REDIS_URL, not REDIS_URL, so the worker may connect to localhost.\n\n12. boot.sh retries forever, boot.sh:3-10. flask db upgrade is retried every 5s with no limit and no error output kept, so a bad migration or bad DATABASE_URL makes the container hang \"running\" instead of failing fast. Also, with DATABASE_URL unset, config.py:11-13 silently falls back to SQLite inside the container, so the migration \"succeeds\" and data is lost when the container goes away. Fix: cap retries (e.g. 10) then exit 1, and require DATABASE_URL in production.\n\n13. Messages page pagination is broken, app/templates/messages.html:11,16. Both links have href=\"#\" and ignore the prev_url/next_url that the route passes. Fix: use {{ prev_url }} and {{ next_url }} as index.html does.\n\n### LOW\n\n14. Fragile javascript: URL in _post.html:21-25. post.language and g.locale are placed inside JS string literals in a javascript: href. HTML escaping doesn't protect here, because entities are decoded before the JS runs. The values are safe today (langdetect output and a whitelisted locale), but any future way to set language (e.g. a posts API) becomes XSS. Fix: use data- attributes and an event listener.\n\n15. Translation strings render as raw HTML, _post.html:14-15. Under newstyle gettext with autoescape the translated string is treated as Markup, so translator-supplied HTML in .po files is rendered unescaped. The interpolated args are still escaped. This is the accepted pattern; just keep .po files trusted.\n\n16. config.py:17 MAIL_USE_TLS: is not None means MAIL_USE_TLS=false or 0 turns TLS on. Parse it as a boolean. Also config.py:20 ADMINS is a placeholder address that is used as the email sender and the error-mail recipient.\n\n17. Logs go to a file inside the container. config.py:14 and app/__init__.py:78-91: unless LOG_TO_STDOUT is set, logs are written to logs/ inside the container, and the Dockerfile doesn't set it. Fix: ENV LOG_TO_STDOUT=1 in the Dockerfile. The legacy ENV FLASK_APP microblog.py syntax at Dockerfile:12 should become ENV FLASK_APP=microblog.py.\n\n18. nginx static location, deployment/nginx/microblog:32-34. location /static without a trailing slash also matches /staticXYZ. It isn't traversable as written, because neither the location nor the alias has a trailing slash. Prefer location /static/ { alias .../app/static/; }.\n\n19. Supervisor configs, deployment/supervisor/*.conf. No stdout/stderr log settings, gunicorn has no access log, and the tasks worker has no stopwaitsecs for in-flight jobs. Minor.\n\n20. Misleading migration docstring, migrations/versions/d049de007ccf_private_messages.py:4. The docstring says Revises: 834b1a697901 but the code (line 14) says down_revision = '2b017edaa91f'. The chain is linear with a single head, 834b1a697901 (verified with flask db history). But if someone \"fixes\" the code to match the docstring, it creates a cycle (834b → c81b → f7ac → d049 → 834b). Fix the docstring.\n\n21. Migration style and indexes.\n    - 834b1a697901_user_tokens.py:21-31 uses plain op.add_column/drop_column while every other migration uses batch_alter_table. Downgrade on SQLite needs 3.35 or later. Use batch mode for consistency.\n    - Down-migrations otherwise correctly mirror their upgrades.\n    - Nullability and lengths match models.py.\n    - Performance: followers.followed_id and task.user_id have no index (in the model or migrations). task is queried on every authenticated page render (base.html:69), and followed_id in follower counts. Consider adding indexes.\n\n22. Translations, app/translations/es/LC_MESSAGES/messages.po:\n    - \"Search Results\" (line 227) has an empty msgstr, so it shows in English.\n    - Three msgids are stale and unused: \"Previous results\", \"Next results\", \"Error: Could not contact server.\" (lines 230-236, 201-203).\n    - All #: location comments are out of date (POT from 2017).\n\n    Every string used in code or templates has an entry. Fix: run flask translate update and fill in the missing translation. Note .mo files are gitignored, so non-Docker deploys (supervisor) must run flask translate compile. Also, app/cli.py:18-39 uses os.system with string concatenation of lang (command injection, but only from a CLI argument, so minimal risk).\n\n23. Template odds and ends.\n    - base.html:2 hardcodes lang=\"en\"; it should be {{ g.locale }}.\n    - The logout link at base.html:60 is a GET, so logout can be triggered cross-site.\n    - In bootstrap_wtf.html:57, hidden-field errors (including a CSRF-token failure) use invalid-feedback, which is display:none without a sibling .is-invalid, so the user never sees them.\n\n    CSRF tokens themselves are present: every POST form uses quick_form (form.hidden_tag()) or explicit hidden_tag() (user.html:22,29, user_popup.html:14,21). Search is GET. The /translate fetch (base.html:101) sends no CSRF token and there's no global CSRFProtect, but get_json() needs application/json, which forces a CORS preflight, so cross-site abuse is unlikely.\n\n24. requirements.txt:37: psycopg2-binary is discouraged for production by its maintainers. Flask-Mail==0.9.1 is old and largely unmaintained. Low.\n\n### No issues found in\n- Vagrantfile: just a box and a private IP, no provisioning.\n- .gitattributes: forces LF on *.sh, which is good for boot.sh.\n- migrations/env.py and alembic.ini: standard Flask-Migrate setup.\n- boot.sh's exec gunicorn: correct.\n- Email templates: autoescaped, and the .txt versions are fine.",
  "Review API and auth": "Code review: auth and REST API of the microblog app at /home/learner/checkout (read-only, no repo files edited)\n\nTests: pytest is not installed in the venv. venv/bin/python tests.py gives 4/4 passing (UserModelCase only). There are no tests for auth or the API. (python -I tests.py cannot import app, because -I leaves the script dir off sys.path.) I also checked the findings below with the Flask test client against a throwaway DB under /tmp, which I deleted afterwards. Where a finding says \"confirmed\", I saw it happen in that run.\n\n## HIGH\n\n1. Hardcoded fallback SECRET_KEY (config.py:9, 'you-will-never-guess')\n   - Problem: If the SECRET_KEY env var is unset, anyone can sign valid JWTs and session cookies.\n   - Scenario: An attacker signs {'reset_password': <id>, 'exp': ...} with the known key, opens /auth/reset_password/<jwt> and sets a new password for any user ID. That is account takeover. They can also forge the Flask session _user_id cookie. Confirmed: a forged reset JWT opened the reset form (200).\n   - Fix: Fail at startup if SECRET_KEY is missing outside dev/testing. Never ship a default.\n\n2. Open redirect via next (app/auth/routes.py:26-29)\n   - Problem: The only check is urlsplit(next).netloc != ''. That misses backslash and scheme-only forms. Confirmed redirects after login:\n     - next=/\\evil.com → Location: /\\evil.com (browsers treat it as //evil.com)\n     - next=\\\\evil.com → \\\\evil.com\n     - next=https:evil.com → https:evil.com\n   - Scenario: Phishing link https://site/auth/login?next=/%5Cevil.com. The user logs in on the real site and lands on the attacker's page.\n   - Fix: Require next to start with a single /, not // or /\\. Reject any backslash, and require an empty scheme and netloc. Or allowlist endpoints / use url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme-style logic.\n\n## MEDIUM\n\n3. Password reset token can be replayed and is not tied to the password (app/models.py:181-193, app/auth/routes.py:72-84)\n   - Problem: The JWT holds only the user id and exp. It stays valid for its full 10 minutes after use. Confirmed: the same token reset the password twice, and the second password took effect.\n   - Scenario: A leaked reset link (mail logs, browser history, Referer to the jsdelivr CDN loaded in base.html:90) can be reused after the victim has already reset.\n   - Fix: Put a fingerprint of the current password_hash (or a per-user nonce) in the token and check it on verify. Use jwt.decode(..., options={\"require\": [\"exp\"]}) and narrow except Exception to jwt.InvalidTokenError.\n\n4. Password reset does not revoke API tokens (app/auth/routes.py:81-82)\n   - Problem: set_password does not call revoke_token(), and nothing invalidates existing sessions or remember-me cookies.\n   - Scenario: A victim resets after a compromise, but the attacker's bearer token stays valid until it expires (up to 1h), and their sessions keep working.\n   - Fix: Call user.revoke_token() on reset. Use a session/alternative-id rotation for Flask-Login.\n\n5. API create_user crashes on malformed input and does no validation (app/api/users.py:47-57, app/models.py:252-257)\n   - 500 errors (confirmed): a JSON null body, a JSON list body ([\"username\",\"email\",\"password\"]), and a non-string password ({\"password\": 1}). The cause is 'x' in None and generate_password_hash(int).\n   - Accepted without validation (confirmed 201): empty username, empty password, and email:\"not-an-email\".\n   - No length or type checks: these fields are limited only by the DB (String(64)/(120)/(140); SQLite doesn't enforce). For example, about_me of 500 chars was accepted.\n   - Scenario: Anyone without auth can create accounts with empty passwords or junk emails, and can trigger 500s.\n   - Fix: Use request.get_json(silent=True) and require a dict. Validate types, lengths, email format and a non-empty password (or reuse a schema/WTForms). Return 400 on failure.\n\n6. API update_user accepts nulls and bad types (app/api/users.py:70-80)\n   - Problem: {\"username\": null} gives a 500 (confirmed; IntegrityError / crash). There is no type, length or email validation, and an email can be changed without re-verification.\n   - Race: The uniqueness checks at lines 71-78 are check-then-act. Two concurrent requests both pass, and the unique index raises IntegrityError, which returns 500 instead of 400. The same race exists in create_user.\n   - Fix: Validate the same way as in #5. Catch IntegrityError, roll back and return 400/409.\n   - Authorization: The A-modifies-B check is correct (users.py:67-68 returns 403 when the token user != id). Mass assignment is properly limited to username/email/about_me, and the password is ignored on update.\n\n7. Unauthenticated email and username enumeration (app/api/users.py:50-55)\n   - Problem: POST /api/users with no auth returns \"please use a different email address\" for registered emails (confirmed). This undoes the enumeration-safe wording in reset_password_request (routes.py:63-66). The registration form does the same (forms.py:26-36).\n   - Timing leaks: verify_password (api/auth.py:13-14) and login (routes.py:22) only run the expensive hash check when the user exists, so response time reveals valid usernames.\n   - Fix: Rate-limit registration. Run a dummy check_password_hash when the user is missing. Consider sending a \"you already have an account\" email instead of an inline error.\n\n8. No rate limiting or lockout on credential endpoints\n   - Where: /api/tokens (Basic auth), /auth/login, /api/users, /auth/reset_password_request.\n   - Scenario: Unlimited password brute force, and reset-email flooding of any address.\n   - Fix: Add Flask-Limiter or similar.\n\n## LOW\n\n9. Pagination metadata reflects raw input (app/api/users.py:19-20 and similar; app/models.py:66-82)\n   - Problem: page=-3&per_page=0 returns 200 with _meta.page=-3, per_page=0. Flask-SQLAlchemy quietly uses page 1 / per_page 20, so the metadata and self link are wrong (confirmed). Negative per_page also passes min(...,100).\n   - Fix: Clamp page>=1 and 1<=per_page<=100, or report resources.page / resources.per_page.\n\n10. API errors not always JSON (app/api/errors.py:17-19)\n    - Problem: Blueprint error handlers don't see routing errors. A 405 on /api/tokens (GET) returned an HTML body (confirmed). App-level handlers (app/errors/handlers.py) only cover 404/500, and use Accept-header sniffing rather than the path.\n    - Smaller issues: handle_exception drops e.description. A 500 from an API route returns JSON only if the client's Accept prefers JSON.\n    - Fix: Add an app-level HTTPException handler that returns JSON when request.blueprint == 'api' or the path starts with /api/.\n\n11. Bearer tokens stored in plaintext (app/models.py:109-110, 264)\n    - Problem: Tokens are stored as is and looked up by equality in SQL (models.py:275).\n    - Scenario: A DB read or backup leak exposes live tokens.\n    - Fix: Store a SHA-256 of the token and look up by the hash. The timing side channel from SQL equality is negligible, so not a concern.\n\n12. get_token returns the existing token without extending it (models.py:261-263)\n    - Problem: If the current token has more than 60s left, the same token is returned with its original expiry. That is correct but surprising: clients that re-auth get a token that may die soon.\n    - Revocation works: confirmed 401 after DELETE /api/tokens, and the next POST issues a new token.\n    - Uncertain: whether this is intended behaviour.\n\n13. check_password crashes when password_hash is NULL (models.py:137-138)\n    - Problem: The column is Optional. check_password_hash(None, ...) raises, so login or Basic auth for such a user returns 500.\n    - Uncertain: No current code path creates users without a password. Guard with if not self.password_hash: return False.\n\n14. Logout via GET (routes.py:33-36)\n    - Problem: A cross-site image tag or link can log users out (CSRF logout). Minor.\n    - Fix: Use POST with a CSRF token.\n\n15. Insecure cookie defaults (config.py)\n    - Problem: SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE, REMEMBER_COOKIE_SECURE and SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE are not set, and the remember-me cookie lasts 365 days by default.\n    - Fix: Set them for production.\n\n16. Case-sensitive username and email uniqueness and lookup (forms.py:27-35, routes.py:61-62, api/users.py:50-55)\n    - Problem: A@x.com and a@x.com can be separate accounts, and a reset request with different casing silently finds no user.\n    - Fix: Normalise to lowercase (at least emails) on write and on lookup.\n\n## Checked and OK\n- Templates: No injection or open redirect in app/templates/auth/* or email/reset_password.*. Jinja autoescape covers user.username in the HTML email. The reset URL is built with url_for(_external=True); behind a proxy without SERVER_NAME it uses the Host header (host-header poisoning of the reset link is possible if the deployment doesn't pin SERVER_NAME or trusted hosts — uncertain, deployment-dependent).\n- Email exposure: to_dict never includes email in API responses.\n- JWT algorithm: It is pinned (algorithms=['HS256']), so no alg-confusion attack.\n- Token generation: secrets.token_hex(16) is adequate."
};
export const SAYS = [
  "Flask microblog app, about 3k lines. I'll split the review across three parallel reviewers by area.",
  "I've started three reviewers in parallel, each covering one part of the codebase (about 3,000 lines, a Flask microblog app):\n\n1. <b>API and authentication:</b> app/api/, app/auth/, token and password handling in models.py, and config.py. They'll look for ways around authentication, missing permission checks, mass assignment and open redirects.\n2. <b>Core app logic:</b> models.py, app/main/routes.py, search, background tasks, translation, CLI and tests.py. They'll look for correctness bugs, slow queries, database session handling, outside services that fail or hang, and gaps in the tests.\n3. <b>Deployment and front end:</b> Dockerfile, boot.sh, nginx and supervisor configs, requirements.txt, migrations and templates. They'll look for insecure defaults, outdated packages, XSS and CSRF problems, and migrations that don't match the models.\n\nWhen all three finish, I'll combine their findings into one prioritised report with file:line references.",
  "The core-logic review is back: 4 tests pass, and it found 4 high-severity issues. The export task can crash before its database record exists, its error handling is broken, Elasticsearch outages cause 500 errors, and the /translate endpoint has no timeout or input checks. The API/auth and deployment/front-end reviews are still running. I'll combine all three into one report when they're done.",
  "The API/auth review is back. Its most serious findings:\n\n- <b>Default secret key:</b> config.py:9 falls back to a hardcoded SECRET_KEY. If the environment variable isn't set, anyone can forge password-reset tokens and session cookies, so they could take over any account.\n- <b>Open redirect:</b> the next check after login (app/auth/routes.py:26-29) lets through values like /\\evil.com, which sends a user to an outside site right after they log in.\n\nThe reviewer confirmed both by running them against a throwaway copy of the database. The deployment and front-end review is the last one still running; I'll write the combined report when it finishes."
];
export const SPOT_CMD = "sed -n 9p config.py; sed -n 26,29p app/auth/routes.py; sed -n 131p app/templates/base.html; sed -n 11,16p app/templates/messages.html; sed -n 209,215p app/models.py";
export const SPOT_OUT = "    SECRET_KEY = os.environ.get('SECRET_KEY') or 'you-will-never-guess'\n        next_page = request.args.get('next')\n        if not next_page or urlsplit(next_page).netloc != '':\n            next_page = url_for('main.index')\n        return redirect(next_page)\n            const response = await fetch('/user/' + ev.target.innerText.trim() + '/popup');\n                <a class=\"page-link\" href=\"#\">\n                    <span aria-hidden=\"true\">&larr;</span> {{ _('Newer messages') }}\n                </a>\n            </li>\n            <li class=\"page-item{% if not next_url %} disabled{% endif %}\">\n                <a class=\"page-link\" href=\"#\">\n    def launch_task(self, name, description, *args, **kwargs):\n        rq_job = current_app.task_queue.enqueue(f'app.tasks.{name}', self.id,\n                                                *args, **kwargs)\n        task = Task(id=rq_job.get_id(), name=name, description=description,\n                    user=self)\n        db.session.add(task)\n        return task";
export const REPLY = "<b>Code review: microblog (Flask)</b>\n\nAll three reviews are done. The 4 existing tests pass, but they only cover the User model; there are no tests for routes, the API, auth, tasks or search. Nothing was changed in the repo. I re-read the code for the top findings myself (secret key, open redirect, popup URL, export race, messages pagination). The reviewers also ran several findings against a throwaway database. Where I write \"confirmed\", it means a reviewer saw it happen in that run.\n\n<b>Fix first (critical/high)</b>\n\n1. <b>Default secret key: anyone could take over any account.</b> config.py:9 falls back to 'you-will-never-guess' if SECRET_KEY isn't set. With that key, anyone can forge a password-reset token or a login session for any user. A forged reset link was confirmed to work. The app should refuse to start without a real key.\n2. <b>Reset links can point at an attacker's site.</b> nginx accepts any hostname (server_name _ plus Host $host, deployment/nginx/microblog:13,27), and Flask builds the link in reset emails from that hostname. An attacker can send a real reset email to a victim whose link points at evil.com and leaks the token. Fix: set an explicit server_name, and set SERVER_NAME or TRUSTED_HOSTS in Flask.\n3. <b>Open redirect after login.</b> The check in app/auth/routes.py:27 only looks at netloc, so next=/\\evil.com and next=https:evil.com get through (confirmed). Accept only paths that start with a single / and contain no backslash.\n4. <b>Dependencies with known vulnerabilities in requirements.txt:</b>\n   - Werkzeug 3.0.1: debugger remote code execution. This is worse because .flaskenv:2 turns on FLASK_DEBUG=1.\n   - Jinja2 3.1.2, gunicorn 21.2.0 (request smuggling), requests 2.31.0, urllib3 2.1.0, idna, certifi, setuptools, dnspython.\n   - aiosmtpd is a dev tool, but it's in the production requirements.\n\n   Upgrade these and add pip-audit to CI.\n5. <b>Usernames can trigger actions through the hover popup.</b> base.html:131 builds the popup URL as '/user/' + innerText + '/popup' without encoding it, and usernames can contain any characters. A user named ../auth/logout? logs out everyone who hovers over their name. Combined with sanitize: false on the popover (base.html:121-122), this also removes a safety net against injected HTML. Fix: build the URL with url_for in a data- attribute, and limit usernames to a safe set of characters.\n6. <b>The export task can fail and block all future exports.</b> models.py:209-215 puts the job on the queue before the Task row is committed. A fast worker can't find the row and crashes, and its error handler crashes the same way. The task then stays \"in progress\" forever. Also in app/tasks.py:\n   - The error path marks the task as complete twice.\n   - It never rolls back the database session.\n   - It sleeps 5 seconds per post, so any user with more than about 36 posts hits the queue's 180-second timeout. The job is then reported as complete and no email is sent.\n\n<b>Medium</b>\n\n<b>Password reset and auth</b>\n- A reset token can be used again until it expires (confirmed). Resetting a password doesn't revoke API tokens or existing sessions (models.py:181-193, auth/routes.py:81).\n- <b>API input validation</b> (api/users.py:47-80):\n  - A null or list body, or a non-string password, causes a 500 (confirmed).\n  - An empty password or an invalid email is accepted (confirmed).\n  - Two simultaneous requests for the same username both pass the uniqueness check, and the second gets a 500 instead of a 400.\n- The API registration error and the login timing both reveal which emails and usernames exist. There's no rate limiting on login, token, registration or reset endpoints.\n- logout and export_posts change state on a plain GET, so another site can trigger them. Session cookies have no Secure or SameSite settings.\n\n<b>Outside services</b>\n- If Elasticsearch is down, a post is saved but the user gets a 500 (models.py:41-51). Search also 500s when ES is down, or on a fresh deploy before the index exists.\n- Search pages past the end produce invalid SQL (CASE id END) and a 500. Page 0 or below sends ES an invalid offset (models.py:22-30).\n- <b>/translate</b> (app/translate.py):\n  - There's no timeout.\n  - The language codes are pasted into the query string unescaped, so a user can add extra target languages and run up translation costs.\n  - There's no limit on text length.\n  - A missing request body causes a 500.\n\n<b>Deployment</b>\n- The Docker container runs as root, uses the floating python:slim tag, and installs some packages unpinned.\n- Procfile uses ;, so gunicorn starts even if the migration fails.\n- boot.sh retries the migration forever. If DATABASE_URL isn't set, it quietly falls back to SQLite inside the container, and the data is lost when the container goes.\n- nginx has no TLS protocol or cipher settings, no HSTS or security headers, and no X-Forwarded-Proto, so reset links are generated as http://.\n- .gitignore doesn't exclude .env, certs/ or *.pem.\n\n<b>Performance</b>\n- Every logged-in request writes and commits last_seen, including notification polling (main/routes.py:16-22).\n- Post lists do N+1 queries for each post's author. followers.followed_id and task.user_id have no index.\n\n<b>Small bugs</b>\n- On the messages page, the Newer/Older links go to href=\"#\" (messages.html:11,16), so you can't page through messages.\n- Datetimes are mixed: some have a timezone and some don't, and the columns don't store one.\n\n<b>Low</b>\n- Bearer tokens are stored in plaintext.\n- check_password crashes if password_hash is NULL.\n- Usernames and emails are case-sensitive, so A@x.com and a@x.com can be separate accounts.\n- MAIL_USE_TLS=false actually turns TLS on.\n- API 405 errors come back as HTML. Pagination _meta echoes invalid page values back.\n- The Spanish translation is missing \"Search Results\" and has stale entries. base.html hardcodes lang=\"en\".\n- The docstring in migration d049de007ccf names the wrong parent migration (the code itself is correct).\n- cli.py builds shell commands with os.system. The input only comes from the operator, so the risk is low.\n\n<b>Suggested order</b>\n1. Make SECRET_KEY required, upgrade the dependencies, and remove FLASK_DEBUG from .flaskenv.\n2. Fix the next redirect check and pin the hostname (nginx server_name plus SERVER_NAME or TRUSTED_HOSTS).\n3. Encode the popup URL, restrict usernames, and make logout and export POST requests.\n4. Fix the export task ordering and its error handling.\n5. Handle Elasticsearch and translator failures without 500s.\n6. Add route and API tests with the Flask test client.\n\nI can start fixing these if you'd like; the first three are small changes.";
